  "sections": [
    {
      "h2": "UK and US yarn names",
      "html": "<p>UK patterns usually say <strong>4-ply, double knit (DK), aran and chunky</strong>. US patterns say fingering, light worsted (DK), worsted (close to aran) and bulky. Always check the recommended needle size and tension (gauge) on the ball band rather than relying on the name.</p>"
    },
    {
      "h2": "Yarn weights at a glance",
      "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>UK name</th><th>US name</th><th>Typical needles</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lace / 2-ply</td><td>Lace</td><td>1.5–2.25mm</td><td>Shawls, fine lace</td></tr><tr><td>4-ply</td><td>Fingering</td><td>2.75–3.25mm</td><td>Socks, baby clothes</td></tr><tr><td>Double knit (DK)</td><td>Light worsted</td><td>3.75–4mm</td><td>Jumpers, hats</td></tr><tr><td>Aran</td><td>Worsted</td><td>4.5–5mm</td><td>Cables, winter knits</td></tr><tr><td>Chunky</td><td>Bulky</td><td>6–8mm</td><td>Quick, cosy knits</td></tr><tr><td>Super chunky</td><td>Super bulky</td><td>9mm+</td><td>Blankets, statement pieces</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Ranges are typical; always follow the ball band and your pattern.</p>"
    },
    {
      "h2": "Fibres",
      "html": "<ul><li><strong>Wool</strong>: warm, elastic and breathable. Superwash wool can be machine washed.</li><li><strong>Cotton</strong>: cool and crisp, good for summer tops and dishcloths, but less stretchy.</li><li><strong>Acrylic</strong>: affordable and easy care, popular for blankets and children’s wear.</li><li><strong>Blends</strong>: wool with nylon is the classic for hard-wearing socks.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "h2": "From hand knitting to hosiery",
      "html": "<p>Hand knitting and machine knitting share a history. The stocking frame, invented in 1589, started an industry that made the East Midlands the home of British hosiery. Read about <a href=\"/leicester-hosiery/\">Leicester’s hosiery heritage</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "h2": "British wool and Leicester sheep",
      "html": "<p>Two famous breeds carry the county’s name. The <strong>Leicester Longwool</strong> was developed by Robert Bakewell at Dishley, near Loughborough, in the 18th century, and the <strong>Bluefaced Leicester</strong> is prized for fine, lustrous wool. Read <a href=\"/blog/leicester-sheep-breeds-british-wool/\">Leicester sheep and British wool</a>.</p><figure class=\"fig \"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1742071853530-bc7446daa0f6?w=1200&q=70&auto=format\" srcset=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1742071853530-bc7446daa0f6?w=700&q=70&auto=format 700w, https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1742071853530-bc7446daa0f6?w=1200&q=70&auto=format 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 760px\" alt=\"Sheep grazing on a grassy hillside\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1200\" height=\"800\"><figcaption>British fleece is collected, graded and sold for UK farmers.<span class=\"fig-credit\">Photo: <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/@veronicaa_white?utm_source=shmor&amp;utm_medium=referral\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">Veronica White</a> / Unsplash</span></figcaption></figure>"
    },
    {
      "h2": "Caring for wool",
      "html": "<ul><li>Follow the ball band: many wools are hand wash only unless marked superwash.</li><li>Wash cool, don’t wring, and dry flat.</li><li>Store clean knits with cedar or lavender to deter moths.</li></ul>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "q": "What is double knit wool?",
      "a": "Double knit (DK) is a medium-weight UK yarn, usually knitted on about 4mm needles. It is the most common weight for jumpers, hats and baby clothes."
    }
  ],
